        "thinking": "There are still `http` imports from non-Express sources:\n1. Line 2257: `import { Agent as hAgent, request as hRequest } from \"http\";` - looks like S3/AWS SDK\n2. Line 14104: `import { request } from \"http\";` - more AWS SDK or similar\n3. Line 84687: `import { createServer } from \"node:http\";` - something else\n\nThese come from packages that are bundled (not aliased). The S3 storage provider uses AWS SDK which imports `http`. The `createServer` from `node:http` is probably from somewhere else.\n\n\nI marked `http` and `node:http` as external in esbuild, which means they're not bundled but instead emit import statements in the output. When the bundle loads in CF Workers, it can't resolve these imports because the runtime doesn't have them available. The question is whether `nodejs_compat` actually provides access to these specific node modules.\n\nLooking at the CF documentation, `nodejs_compat` only supports a limited set of Node.js APIs like `node:buffer`, `node:crypto`, `node:path`, and `node:util` — but critically NOT `node:http`, `node:https`, `node:net`, or `node:fs`. So I need to either bundle stub implementations of these modules or remove the code that imports them entirely.\n\nI'm thinking the real issue is that `api.ts` is importing all server routes indiscriminately, which pulls in Node.js-specific dependencies like the S3 SDK, postgres drivers, and multer that were never meant for a Cloudflare Worker. The Worker should only be using specific routes, not the entire server codebase. The `assetRoutes` serves as a fallback for read operations, but it pulls in `multer` which depends on Node.js APIs like `node:fs` and `node:stream`. I'm weighing two approaches: either stub out all the Node.js modules that get imported (with errors if they're actually called), or be more surgical and exclude `assetRoutes` from the CF bundle entirely since file uploads already use R2-native handlers instead.\n\nLooking at what the CF worker actually needs, the asset routes handle non-upload operations like GET requests and logo retrieval, but the multer-dependent upload routes won't work in CF anyway. The real problem is that bundling `assetRoutes` pulls in multer regardless. The simplest solution is to not bundle `assetRoutes` in the first place.\n\nBut if we do include it, I should mark multer as external so it doesn't get bundled—the import will fail at runtime if someone tries that code path, but CF never reaches it since it has R2-native handlers. The S3 SDK has the same issue: it's only used by the S3 storage provider, which CF doesn't use. I need to track down what's pulling in the `http` module on line 84687 of the bundle to see if there are other dependencies I should exclude.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
